Implement this exact API.

```rust
use std::ops::Range;
#[derive(Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum RangeError { FirstInvalid, SecondInvalid, Overlap }
pub fn select_regions<'a>(buffer: &'a mut [u8], first: Range<usize>, second: Range<usize>)
    -> Result<(&'a mut [u8], &'a mut [u8]), RangeError>;
```

Ranges are half-open: start is included and end is excluded. A range is invalid if start > end or end > buffer.len(). Check FirstInvalid first, then SecondInvalid, then Overlap only if both ranges are valid. Two nonempty ranges overlap when first.start < second.end and second.start < first.end. Preserve argument order. An empty range contains no elements and cannot overlap another range. For example, 2..2 and 0..4 can coexist, as can two empty ranges.

Leave the buffer unchanged on error. On success, every nonempty slice must refer to the exact original elements and have the exact requested length. Tuple order always follows argument order, even when second lies earlier in the buffer. Changes through the slices must be visible after their borrows end, leaving any gap and the rest of the buffer untouched. Adjacent ranges and ranges separated by a gap are valid. Nested and crossing nonempty ranges overlap. The tests check this behavior and allow any correct choice of syntax, allocation strategy, or safe implementation without requiring unsafe. For empty results, pointer identity is unconstrained and length must be zero.

